{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli Summary**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. In 1164 he was captured at the Battle of Harim and held captive by Nur ad-Din for almost ten years until he was ransomed in 1174.\n\n**highlighted section part 1**\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states during the 12th century. He was a key figure in the faction opposing the expansionist policies of Amalric I of Jerusalem. After Amalric’s death in 1174 Raymond served as bailli of Jerusalem during the minority of Baldwin IV. He was instrumental in securing the coronation of Baldwin V in 1185 and acted as regent until the young king’s death in 1186. Raymond III also mediated disputes between various noble factions and worked to maintain unity among the Crusader states.\n\n**highlighted section part 2**\n\nRaymond III’s reign was marked by ongoing conflict with Saladin. In 1187 Saladin launched a major invasion of the Crusader states. Raymond III initially opposed confronting Saladin directly but was overruled by other Crusader leaders. The Crusaders suffered a crushing defeat at the Battle of Hattin on July 4 1187. Raymond III escaped the battle but died shortly afterward on February 10 1187. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli as the Crusader states faced increasing pressure from Saladin and his successors.\n\n**highlighted section part 3**\n\nRaymond III was married to Eschiva of Bures with whom he had no children. His death without an heir led to a succession crisis in Tripoli. The county eventually passed to his cousin Raymond IV of Toulouse. Raymond III’s legacy i